# Claim 1

**1.** A computer-implemented method for generating a collision-free tool path for a movable tool, the method comprising:

**(a)** receiving an initial tool path defined by a plurality of waypoints connected by one or more straight-line segments extending between a start configuration and an end configuration;

**(b)** optimizing the initial tool path into a polynomial trajectory by fitting at least one polynomial function to at least a subset of the plurality of waypoints, the polynomial trajectory defining a continuous path of the tool as a function of a path parameter;

**(c)** performing a collision check on the polynomial trajectory against a model of an environment to determine whether the polynomial trajectory intersects one or more obstacles represented in the model;

**(d)** in response to determining that the polynomial trajectory intersects at least one of the one or more obstacles, modifying the polynomial trajectory to reduce or eliminate the intersection, and iteratively repeating the collision check of step (c) on the modified polynomial trajectory; and

**(e)** outputting the polynomial trajectory as the collision-free tool path when the collision check indicates that the polynomial trajectory is free of intersection with the one or more obstacles.

---

### Notes on drafting choices

- **Preamble scope** — I framed it broadly as a "movable tool" (covers machine tools, robot end-effectors, additive/subtractive manufacturing heads). You may want to narrow this to your specific application if the field is well-defined.
- **Step (b) "at least a subset"** — allows for the trajectory to be fit to fewer than all waypoints (useful for smoothing/corner-cutting embodiments).
- **The iterative loop** is captured in step (d) by "iteratively repeating."

Consider adding **dependent claims** covering, e.g.:
- specific polynomial types (B-splines, Bézier, cubic/quintic splines);
- constraints during optimization (velocity, acceleration, jerk limits);
- how the trajectory is modified (adding intermediate waypoints, adjusting control points);
- convergence/termination criteria for the iteration.

Would you like me to draft the dependent claims or tailor claim 1 to a particular domain (e.g., robotics, CNC machining)?
